'''Alhambra''' ({{Langx|ar|الْحَمْرَاء|al-Ḥamrāʼ|the red one}}) is the capital of the [[Algarves]], a [[Regions of Balisca|region]] of [[Estuaria]], within the [[Baliscan Confederation]]. It is situated on the island of [[Nueva Mayurqa]]. The harbour of Alhambra has long been a leading tourist destination in Balisca, receiving more than a million tourists through its main port in 2017, and was historically a prominent destination for commercial and {{W|Ocean liners|trans-Atlantic passenger cruises}} between Europe, South America and North Africa. It is one of the most famous monuments to {{W|Mudéjar art|Mudéjar architecture}} and one of the best-preserved displays of the historic Ibero-Mozarabic culture in the world.

The foundation stone of the city was laid by {{W|Mozarab}}s expelled from the [[W:Iberian Peninsula|Iberian Peninsula]] and resettled by the [[Crown of Aragon]] on 28 March 1526, for what later became the [[Great Mosque of Alhambra|Gran Mezquita de Alhambra]]. Following territorial reorganizations by Spanish authorities, Alhambra was selected as seat of the newly created [[Colony of Nueva Córdoba]]. The city is one of the [[W:list of oldest continuously inhabited cities#America|oldest continually inhabited cities in Balisca]].

Like most cities of Aragonese colonial background, the city has a long, multicultural past, and this has been reflected in the city's distinct culture: Alhambra has developed its own unique music, a world famous cuisine (to include the staple {{W|couscous|Cuscús}}), unique dialects, and its annual celebrations and festivals, most notably [[W:Mawlid|Mawlid]] (and related ''[[W:Mehfil|mehfil]]''). 

